# Hackday Development Rules

## Context
This is the OEF Hackdays monorepo. Each team works in `apps/<team-name>/`. Projects are 24-hour builds focused on revenue-enabling tools for climate finance.

## Principles
- Ship over polish. Ugly but working beats beautiful but broken.
- Every app must have a README.md that serves as both documentation and demo script.
- Prefer simple stacks. Next.js, plain HTML, Streamlit, FastAPI — whatever gets you to a demo fastest.
- Use Claude for code generation, design decisions, and debugging. Document what worked and what didn't.

## Code Style
- TypeScript for frontend apps (strict mode).
- Python for backend/data apps (type hints, ruff formatting).
- No complex build systems. Keep it deployable with `npm run dev` or `python app.py`.

## Git Workflow
- Each team works on branch `hackday/<team-name>`.
- Commit early and often. Messy history is fine for a hackday.
- Open a PR to `main` before demo time with a description of what you built.

## Data & APIs
- CityCatalyst Global API is available for city data and emissions.
- Use public data sources. Don't commit API keys — use `.env.local`.
- If you need OEF-internal data, ask Pablo or check the `cc-poc-template` repo for OAuth setup.
